steps.body:
A feed handler reads a stream of messages, each starting with a type tag: `A` adds an order, `X` cancels one, `T` reports a trade. The usual designs are a hand-kept `switch`, or a base class with a virtual `handle`, which costs an indirect call per message and keeps the compiler from inlining (the lesson on polymorphism costs, later in the track, measures that). A variadic template can write the switch for you.

Give each message type a `static constexpr char tag` and a static `read` function that reads its fields from a `std::istream&`, a reference to any input stream, `std::cin` included. List the types once, as a pack, and fold over `||`:

```cpp harness
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>

struct Trade {
    static constexpr char tag = 'T';
    std::int64_t qty = 0;
    static Trade read(std::istream& in) { Trade m; in >> m.qty; return m; }
};

struct Volume {
    std::int64_t total = 0;
    void on(const Trade& t) { total += t.qty; }
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    std::istringstream feed("40");
    Volume v;
    std::cout << dispatch<Trade>('T', feed, v) << ' ' << dispatch<Trade>('Q', feed, v) << ' ' << v.total << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename... Msgs, typename Handler>
bool dispatch(char tag, std::istream& in, Handler& handler) {
    return ((tag == Msgs::tag && (handler.on(Msgs::read(in)), true)) || ...);
}
```

`dispatch<AddOrder, Cancel, Trade>` fills `Msgs` with the three types and deduces `Handler`. The fold becomes three tests joined by `||`, which stops at the first true one. Inside each test, `(handler.on(...), true)` uses the comma operator, which runs its left side, drops the result, and yields its right side: the handler runs, then the test is `true`. An unknown tag fails every test, so `dispatch` returns `false`.

Each `handler.on(...)` gets an exact type, so the compiler picks which `on` to call while compiling, the way it picks any overload. There's no table and no virtual call, and a missing `on(const Trade&)` is a compile error rather than a message dropped in production. Here's g++'s `-O2` output for `feed`, which calls a version of `dispatch<AddOrder, Cancel, Trade>` that reads one number from its arguments instead of a stream (65, 88, and 84 are `'A'`, `'X'`, and `'T'`):

```text
_Z4feedclR10FeedCounts:
	cmp	dil, 65
	je	.L7
	cmp	dil, 88
	je	.L8
	xor	eax, eax
	cmp	dil, 84
	je	.L9
	ret
.L8:
	add	QWORD PTR [rdx+8], 1
	mov	eax, 1
	ret
...
```

Three compares, and each handler inlined to a single `add`.

